// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// A value the attacker can only hold because the very receive it is attacking
// already succeeded is not available at that receive. Alice signs an early
// challenge and a late one over the same guarded nonce; Bob checks the early
// signature, and only then does he send ok, and only then does Alice compute
// the late pair. Splicing late into early makes every one of Bob's checks
// pass, and it is still impossible: the cycle is Bob accepts late as early ->
// Bob sends ok -> Alice computes late.
//
// No sibling run supplies it either. na is guarded, so the attacker cannot
// put session 1's nonce into session 2's ASSERT, and Alice's signature over
// na fails a run expecting na#2. Bob checks the nonce before the signature
// for that reason: reversed, a sibling run's flight verifies before Bob halts
// on the nonce, which is a genuine injectivity failure of a different kind
// and would make this model pin two things at once.
//
// exa2.vp and kem_direction_reflection.vp are the counterweights: there the
// reflected value is on the wire before the receive it is fed into, so it is
// causally available and the attack is real.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private a
	knows public first, second
	ga = PUBKEY(a)
	generates na
	early_msg = CONCAT(na, first)
	early = SIGN(a, early_msg)
]

Alice -> Bob: [ga], [na], early_msg, early

principal Bob[
	generates ok
	x, label = SPLIT(early_msg)?
	_ = ASSERT(x, na)?
	_ = SIGNVERIF(ga, early_msg, early)?
]

Bob -> Alice: [ok]

principal Alice[
	late_msg = CONCAT(na, second)
	late = SIGN(a, late_msg)
]

Alice -> Bob: late_msg, late

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: early
]
